Tags 

.<p> 
.defines a paragraph of text 
.automatically adds space before and after the content
.block-level element 
.browsers automatically add line breaks and spacing before and after each tags 
.due to browsers adding default styling to <p> tags, override them with CSS

<a>/ link tag attributes
        attributes:                     description
        href                            specifies the URL of the page the link goes to
        target                          specifies where to open the linked document 
        title                           specifies extra information about the link (displayed as a tooltip when the mouse hovers over the link)
        rel                             specifies the relationship between the current document and the linked document (e.g., "
        media                           specifies the media type of the linked document (e.g., "screen", "print")
        hreflang                        specifies the language of the linked document (e.g., "en" for English, "fr" for French)
        download                        specifies that the linked document should be downloaded instead of opened in the browser (can also specify a filename for the downloaded file)
        charset                         specifies the character encoding of the linked document (e.g., "UTF-8")
        name                            specifies a name for the anchor (used for linking to specific sections within the same page)
        shape                           specifies the shape of the clickable area for an image link (e.g., "rect", "circle", "poly")
        rev                             specifies the reverse relationship between the current document and the linked document (e.g., "author", "bookmark")

<div>
.defines a division or a section in an HTML document
.used as a container for HTML elements to style them with CSS or to perform certain tasks with JavaScript
.block-level element
    .can behave like an inline element while retaining block-level properties

.use classes and IDs to style <div> elements with CSS

<header>
.defines the introductory content of a document or a section
.can contain headings, logos, navigation links, search bar, and other introductory elements
.cannot be placed within <footer>, <address>, or <header> elements
.doesn't have attributes of its own, but can use global attributes - id, class, style, etc.
.it also supports event attributes like onclick, onmouseover, etc. for interactivity
.can be multiple <header> elements in a document, but they should be used to group introductory content for different sections of the page
    